  170 => [
    'transcript' => 'Baroque architects of the seventeenth century, most notably Gian Lorenzo Bernini in Rome, sought to engage viewers emotionally through dramatic spatial effects rather than the calm, balanced proportions favored during the Renaissance. Curving facades, dynamic colonnades, and elaborate sculptural decoration were combined to create buildings that seemed to move and breathe. Bernini\'s colonnade encircling St. Peter\'s Square, for example, uses converging rows of columns to create the sensation of being embraced upon entering the space. Such designs were intended to overwhelm the senses and evoke a powerful feeling of ...',
