  171 => [
    'transcript' => 'In a binary star system, a dense white dwarf can gradually pull material from its companion star, slowly increasing its own mass over time. Once the white dwarf approaches a well-defined mass limit, it becomes unstable and undergoes a runaway thermonuclear explosion, destroying the star entirely in a brilliant supernova. Because this explosion always occurs at nearly the same critical mass, these events release remarkably consistent amounts of light, allowing astronomers to calculate precisely how far away they are based on how bright they appear. For this reason, astronomers rely on these explosions as reliable ...',
