  178 => [
    'transcript' => 'Nearly everyone has experienced the frustrating sensation of knowing a word exists, sensing its approximate length or number of syllables, yet being momentarily unable to retrieve it. Psycholinguists label this the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon and use it as evidence that word meaning and word form are stored somewhat separately in the mental lexicon. During these episodes, speakers often successfully recall partial information, such as the item\'s first letter or its general sense, while the complete phonological shape remains inaccessible. Because meaning can apparently be activated without triggering full retrieval of sound, researchers argue this phenomenon reveals a genuine gap between accessing what we mean to say and accessing how to actually ...',
