  180 => [
    'transcript' => 'In 1964, the murder of Kitty Genovese in New York City, reportedly witnessed by numerous nearby residents who did not intervene, prompted psychologists to investigate why individuals often fail to help a person in distress when other people are present. Subsequent laboratory experiments confirmed that as the number of onlookers at an emergency increases, each individual becomes less likely to take action, partly because responsibility for helping feels diffused across the group and partly because people look to others\' inaction as a cue that intervention may be unnecessary. This paradoxical decrease in individual helping behavior as group size grows is known as the ...',
