  181 => [
    'transcript' => 'Structural functionalism, associated with theorists such as Emile Durkheim and Talcott Parsons, views society as a system whose institutions work together to maintain stability. From this view, even phenomena that appear harmful, such as a modest level of crime, may serve a latent function by reinforcing collective moral boundaries. Conflict theorists, drawing on Karl Marx, sharply reject this harmonious picture, arguing that institutions primarily protect the interests of powerful groups at the expense of the disadvantaged. Where functionalists see cooperation and shared values, conflict theorists see competition over scarce resources such as wealth and power. These two schools therefore offer fundamentally different explanations for why inequality and institutional arrangements persist despite ongoing ...',
