  183 => [
    'transcript' => 'Georges Seurat developed a painting technique in the 1880s that applied color not through blended brushstrokes but through thousands of small, distinct dots of pure pigment placed carefully side by side. Rather than mixing colors on the palette, Seurat relied on the viewer\'s eye to blend adjacent dots optically when observed from an appropriate distance. This method, grounded in contemporary scientific theories of color perception, produced luminous effects that traditional blending could not achieve. Up close, a Seurat canvas appears as a field of individual specks, but from across a gallery room the dots resolve into ...',
