  184 => [
    'transcript' => 'In the late 1990s, two independent teams of astronomers set out to measure how the expansion of the universe was slowing down due to the mutual gravitational attraction of all the matter within it. By observing distant supernovae, they expected to find evidence of gradual deceleration. Instead, their measurements showed that the most distant supernovae were fainter, and therefore farther away, than expected if expansion were merely slowing. This surprising result implied that some mysterious force was overpowering gravity on cosmic scales, pushing galaxies apart at an ever-increasing rate, a phenomenon now attributed to ...',
