  186 => [
    'transcript' => 'Traditional manufacturers often kept large stockpiles of raw materials and components on hand to guard against supply disruptions, tying up significant funds in warehouse space and unused inventory. An alternative approach, pioneered by Japanese automakers in the mid-twentieth century, instead coordinates closely with suppliers so that materials arrive at the factory only shortly before they are needed in production, minimizing storage costs and waste. While this method dramatically improves efficiency under normal conditions, it also leaves manufacturers highly vulnerable to even minor disruptions anywhere in the supply chain. This lean approach to inventory management is widely known as ...',
