  259 => [
    'transcript' => 'Secularization theory, prominent among sociologists throughout much of the twentieth century, predicted that as societies modernized and scientific rationality expanded, religious belief and institutional participation would steadily decline. Proponents pointed to falling church attendance and weakening religious authority in Western Europe as strong supporting evidence for this trajectory. However, the continued vitality of religious practice in the United States, along with the global growth of movements such as Pentecostalism in parts of Africa and Latin America, has led many contemporary sociologists to question whether secularization is truly universal and inevitable. Some scholars now argue that modernization does not eliminate religion so much as transform its role, reshaping how individuals express and experience ...',
