  271 => [
    'transcript' => 'Nobel laureate Daniel Kahneman popularized a framework describing two distinct modes of thinking that operate within the human mind. One mode is fast, automatic, and effortless, generating intuitive judgments and snap reactions with minimal conscious deliberation, such as recognizing a familiar face or completing a simple arithmetic problem. The other mode is slow, deliberate, and effortful, engaged when a person must reason through unfamiliar or complex problems, weigh evidence carefully, or override an initial intuitive impulse. Kahneman argued that many cognitive errors arise when the fast, intuitive mode produces a judgment that goes unchecked by the slower, more analytical mode. This influential framework distinguishing the two modes of cognition is commonly referred to as ...',
