  272 => [
    'transcript' => 'Sociologists studying the modern family have documented a significant shift away from the extended, multigenerational households that once predominated in agrarian societies. Industrialization and urban migration encouraged smaller, more geographically mobile family units, giving rise to what researchers term the nuclear family, typically consisting of parents and their dependent children living apart from other relatives. More recently, sociologists have observed further diversification, with rising rates of single-parent households, blended families formed through remarriage, and cohabiting couples who forgo formal marriage altogether. This growing variety has prompted scholars to reconsider earlier assumptions that treated the nuclear family as a universal, natural standard, arguing instead that family form is a historically contingent product of shifting economic and cultural ...',
