    'transcript' => 'Most plants rely on a photosynthetic pathway that becomes markedly inefficient under hot, dry conditions, since the enzyme responsible for fixing carbon dioxide increasingly binds oxygen instead, wasting energy in a process called photorespiration. Certain plants, including corn and sugarcane, have evolved an alternative pathway, known as C4 photosynthesis, in which carbon dioxide is first fixed in specialized outer cells before being shuttled into protected inner bundle sheath cells for the remaining steps. This spatial separation keeps carbon dioxide concentrations high around the enzyme, sharply reducing wasteful photorespiration. As a result, C4 plants maintain high photosynthetic efficiency even under conditions of intense heat and ...',
